==============================================================================================================
Case 10 [control]: weights change nothing when the critic's error does not grow with the current (ReBRAC; shuffled weights)
==============================================================================================================
Before = standard dose, after = IW dose, four-learner study (exploratory). ReBRAC's critic 'did not learn' (its
standard band already covered 90-96% under the shift); the shuffled-weight placebo keeps the weights' sizes but
breaks their link to the rows.
  rebrac  medium         dose change: strong weights [0.003, -0.006, -0.022, -0.005, 0.003]  shuffled placebo [0.001, 0.003, 0.001, -0.008, 0.006]  estimated weights [0.004, -0.008, -0.020, -0.006, 0.004]
  cql     medium         dose change: strong weights [0.144, 0.147, 0.138, 0.149, 0.128]  shuffled placebo [0.007, -0.007, 0.017, 0.001, -0.006]  estimated weights [0.145, 0.148, 0.138, 0.150, 0.129]
  cql     medium-expert  dose change: strong weights [0.131, 0.156, 0.125, 0.141, 0.131]  shuffled placebo [0.017, -0.008, 0.010, 0.009, -0.001]  estimated weights [0.131, 0.156, 0.122, 0.143, 0.132]
  Count: ReBRAC's change is -0.022 to +0.003 (2 of 5 above 0); CQL's shuffled placebo -0.008 to +0.017
  (6 of 10 above 0) against +0.125 to +0.156 with the real weights. Takeaway: the rise needs weights that
  point at rows where the critic's error is larger; the sample-size part alone is about 0.01 in the dose.

